The want: the browser and an agent as the whole system to work in, with
fewer clicks. An idea, not a plan; nothing here is built.

The shape

    quark  ──http──>  browser       shows: md, pdf, html, py outline
    (≈ 9P)            (≈ rio/acme)
    unix tools, tmux, fm, agent     do: edit, run

Plan 9's shape kept: one plain way to serve files, one general viewer.
The browser is heavy, but so is the kernel, and neither is ours to write.

Two lines

- The browser shows, the terminal does. Reading and moving around fit
  the browser; editing and running fight it (ctrl-w closes the tab).
- Files hold the state, the page holds none: path.md, done, fm's cur.
  A page that keeps its own state has become an app.

The terminal pane

t on a folder: the right pane becomes a shell standing there, `tmux new
-A` under ttyd, in an iframe. One more view, like pdf.js, not a new
structure. fm, ed, c and tmux work in it as they are; nothing is
rewritten as a page. ttyd is not installed. It listens on 127.0.0.1 with
a token, for any page you visit can reach localhost; the tablet the same.

The test, before any of it: for a week, note why you leave the browser.
Mostly "run a command in this folder" and the pane earns its place;
mostly editing and it does not.

An agent

A tool that does, so it sits with the terminal: in a tmux pane, writing
files the page shows. That works today: one agent drives another's pane
by tmux send-keys, the brief between them a file. The browser could add
asking about the row under the cursor, the answer a file beside it.

A bridge

fm writes cur after every ls (`todo/next:/fm opens on cur/`). Shelf
could follow it: fm acts, shelf shows where fm stands. A link, not a merge.

